{
  "dom_id": "step-q35835",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Find the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{-5{x}^{7}+1}{10{x}^{3}+x+3}.$",
  "worked_solution": "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-5{x}^{7}+1}{10{x}^{3}+x+3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(-\\frac{5{x}^{7}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{10{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(-5{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(10+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-5{x}^{4}+0}{10+0+0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote.",
